Using proton-proton collision data collected at a center-of-mass energy of 13 TeV, an examination of three-massive-gauge-boson production is conducted. The dataset, with a total integrated luminosity of 137 fb$^{-1}$, is analyzed for signatures of W bosons and Z bosons in specific decay modes: $\mathrm{e}^{\pm}
u_{\mathrm{e}}$, $\mu^{\pm}
u_{\mu}$, and $\mathrm{e}^{+}\mathrm{e}^{-}
u_{\overline{\mathrm{e}}}
u_{\mathrm{e}}$. The study focuses on final states containing one, two, or three leptons with masses greater than 20 GeV, categorized by the number of jets present. The results are compared to the Standard Model predictions for direct and indirect production of these bosons. The observed data align with Standard Model expectations, with upper limits on the cross-sections for various processes determined. The study also places constraints on the parameters of a model involving a heavy neutral Higgs boson decaying into two Z bosons, with a best limit of 0.0010 pb for a 125 GeV Higgs boson.
